{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "74",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her 50s who was a never-smoker presented with an enlarged, firm right ala. She first noticed a lesion of the right nasal vestibule mucosa 5 years prior. At that time, the lesion was biopsied and reported as a solid and cystic hidradenoma that was p63 positive, pancytokeratin positive, and p16 negative. She noticed recurrence of the lesion 8 months prior to presentation. The lesion was excised but recurred quickly after 2 months.\n\nExamination revealed an enlarged, firm right ala that appeared thicker compared to the left ala. Visualization showed tumor within the nasal vestibule floor and ala.\n\n- Pathology (Initial biopsy): Solid and cystic hidradenoma, p63 positive, pancytokeratin positive, and p16 negative- Pathology (Second excision): p63 positive and p16 negative, with positive margins- Imaging:  Image Title: Figure 1A - Preoperative view  Image Description: Thicker right ala compared with the left ala    Image Title: Figure 1B - Intraoperative view  Image Description: Reflected ala after lateral rhinotomy showing tumor in ala and its extension into the floor of the nasal vestibule    Image Title: Figure 2 - Microscopic examination  Image Description: Downward papillomatous proliferation of small, uniform, slightly atypical keratinocytes within loose stroma (frozen section, hematoxylin-eosin stain, original magnification ×20)\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Squamous cell carcinoma\nB. Inverted Papilloma\nC. Malignant hidradenoma (hidradenocarcinoma)\nD. Nonkeratinizing squamous cell carcinoma of the nasal mucosa with Schneiderian features\n",
